\p{How can the transports used to compare neighboring fields themselves
fluctuate? A [connection background](fdip-006C) assigns a fixed transport
to each edge; it is already gauge-field data. A law on those transports
makes them dynamical. We now sum over them as field variables, while keeping
the graph, its square faces and the group fixed. The faces are extra
incidence data: the earlier graph alone does not specify them. A chosen
unitary representation gives a Wilson action and a finite probability
law with an explicit counting-measure convention.}

\p{Matter integration already distinguishes connections in the
[massive complex Gaussian on a supplied circle](fdip-006I).
Its normalized holonomy action #{W} is minimized at trivial holonomy,
so the integrated matter factor #{e^{-W}} is largest there. That
comparison leaves the circle and connection supplied. The finite law
here is a further choice; transport alone does not derive a force such
as electromagnetism.}

\p{Gauge averaging first determines which parts of an observable can
contribute to its expectation. For the two-element group on a square
torus, all plaquette signs can then be counted exactly. One global
constraint and two remaining cycle holonomies account for the partition
function's normalization. The resulting loop formula gives a pure-gauge
area law in two dimensions; no matter field is included in that result.}

